# Copy to packages/api/.env for local development. Every value below is optional — the API
# boots and the admin portal works with none of them set, using local placeholders (see the
# comments on each). Fill in real values as you set up each service; nothing here needs to
# be set all at once.

# --- Auth --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Without either of these, POST /api/auth/login answers 503 and the portal can't be signed
# into. `npm run hash-password` (from the repo root) prints a value for ADMIN_PASSWORD_HASH.
# For local placeholder use, ADMIN_PASSWORD (plaintext) is the fastest way to get logged in;
# switch to the hash before deploying anywhere real.
ADMIN_PASSWORD=changeme
# ADMIN_PASSWORD_HASH=

# Required for login to work at all — any long random string. Generate one with, e.g.,
# `node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"`.
JWT_SECRET=local-dev-placeholder-change-before-deploying

# --- Firestore -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Leave all three unset to use an in-memory content store instead — genuinely usable for
# local development (the admin portal works end to end), it just resets every time this
# process restarts. Set all three together to use a real Firestore project.
# FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID=
# FIREBASE_CLIENT_EMAIL=
# FIREBASE_PRIVATE_KEY=
# Only if the Firestore database is not the one named `(default)`. A project can hold
# several, and the Admin SDK talks to `(default)` unless told otherwise — so a database
# created under any other id reads as unreachable while everything else looks healthy.
# FIREBASE_DATABASE_ID=

# --- Cloudinary ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The site's build-time images already use an unsigned upload preset (see
# packages/web/src/lib/images.ts) — that's not enough for the admin portal's server-side
# uploads, which need a real signed API key. Generate one in the Cloudinary dashboard under
# Settings → Access Keys, for the same cloud name (o5hr8kjc) the rest of the site uses.
# Format: cloudinary://<api_key>:<api_secret>@<cloud_name>
# Leave unset and image/CV upload answers 503 — everything else still works.
# CLOUDINARY_URL=

# --- CORS ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Comma-separated list of origins allowed to call this API, in addition to localhost:3210
# (the web dev server) which is always allowed. Needs the live site's real origin before
# deploying — e.g. https://qalor.nl
# CORS_ORIGIN=

# --- Misc ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PORT=4000
# HEALTH_DEPS_TIMEOUT_MS=5000
